@@ -0,0 +1,100 @@
+# Nonblocking TCP byte stream
+
+This packet implements the existing `peer::ByteStream` interface with POSIX TCP
+on **Linux and macOS**. It creates no node, peer manager, chainstate, defaults,
+seeds, DNS resolver, profile selection or network activation. Root owns shared
+build registration; unsupported platforms must omit this implementation until a
+separately reviewed adapter exists.
+
+## Explicit API and ownership
+
+`ParseNumericEndpoint` accepts bounded IPv4/IPv6 literals through `inet_pton`,
+with caller-supplied port and IPv6 scope ID. Host names, embedded NUL, bracketed
+URLs and textual zone IDs are rejected. `TcpEndpoint{}` is Unspecified and cannot
+bind/connect. A wildcard address is possible only if explicitly supplied by the
+caller. Connect rejects port0; Listen accepts explicit port0 for OS ephemeral
+allocation. Backlog and IPv6-only policy are explicit arguments. No SO_REUSEPORT
+or SO_REUSEADDR option is silently installed.
+
+`TcpStream` and `TcpListener` each own at most one descriptor. They are move-only;
+move assignment deliberately releases the old owned descriptor. Failed
+BeginConnect/Listen on an open object preserves that connection/listener.
+Accept performs at most one accept syscall, refuses an open stream output before
+accepting, and publishes stream/remote outputs only after complete configuration.
+Failure and WouldBlock leave both outputs unchanged. BoundEndpoint similarly
+changes output only on success. NativeHandle is borrowed: never close it or
+change its nonblocking/close-on-exec flags.
+
+The existing Transport borrows the **stream object**, so never move, replace or
+destroy it while a Transport still references it. Destroy Transport first, or
+close it and keep the referenced stream object alive through Transport destruction.
+Transport cleanup and the stream's destructor are safe together because Close
+exchanges the descriptor to -1 before its single close syscall. Close never
+retries EINTR: POSIX close interruption can be ambiguous, and retrying a reused
+number could close another resource. Kernel disposition after such an ambiguous
+close is outside the close-once guarantee.
+
+Callers serialize access. No callbacks or heap buffers are stored by this adapter.
+On macOS close-on-exec/nonblocking configuration follows socket/accept through
+fcntl; callers must serialize concurrent fork/exec with that configuration
+window. Linux socket/accept4 also set those flags atomically. This packet installs
+no process-wide signal disposition. SIGPIPE is suppressed per send with Linux
+MSG_NOSIGNAL, or per socket with macOS SO_NOSIGPIPE before ownership publication.
+
+## Bounds and operation results
+
+Read/Write issue one syscall with span size capped at SSIZE_MAX. Partial progress
+is returned exactly. EINTR/EAGAIN/EWOULDBLOCK become WouldBlock with count0;
+EOF/Error also have count0. Empty spans or disconnected use produce Error.
+EOF alone retains descriptor ownership until explicit Close or destruction;
+Transport handles its own EOF cleanup. LastError is the latest operational POSIX
+errno, not protocol/consensus invalidity or a peer reputation decision.
+
+PollReadiness checks one descriptor with poll(timeout0). It does not sleep or
+implement a scheduler. BeginConnect and Listen perform a fixed sequence of at
+most ten syscalls, including configuration and failure cleanup; Accept at most
+seven. PollConnect performs at most poll+getsockopt+failure close. None loops on
+interruption. Connect completion
+uses SO_ERROR and a fixed caller-injected monotonic deadline. Zero/overflowing
+timeouts reject before creation; clock rollback closes a pending attempt;
+expiry closes it without extending for retries. Established streams have no
+adapter idle timeout; Session/Transport retain their separate deadlines.
+
+The caller must bound retained stream/listener objects, accept/connect attempts
+per scheduler turn, and transport action/byte budgets. Kernel backlog/socket
+buffers, caller-owned spans and Transport/Session queues have separate quotas.
+The explicit backlog is subject to OS clamping; no exact kernel-memory bound is
+claimed. This packet adds no unbounded descriptor collection or buffering.
+
+## Standalone checks
+
+```sh
+cmake -S src/lotus_v2/peer_tcp_test -B /tmp/lotus-v2-tcp-build -G Ninja -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug -DLOTUS_SECP_LIBRARY=/absolute/path/libsecp256k1.a
+cmake --build /tmp/lotus-v2-tcp-build
